1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,764
Chai Corner has six people now,
and Ravi loves one way to manage.

2
00:00:05,064 --> 00:00:08,125
Long team chats, where
everyone shares ideas.

3
00:00:08,675 --> 00:00:11,124
On a slow afternoon, it works beautifully.

4
00:00:11,424 --> 00:00:15,349
But at eight in the morning, the rush
hits, and Ravi calls a team chat.

5
00:00:15,649 --> 00:00:18,565
Thirty customers wait,
and their chai goes cold.

6
00:00:19,215 --> 00:00:22,953
A week later, it is festival day,
and the queue is even longer.

7
00:00:23,302 --> 00:00:25,651
This time, Ravi does not call a chat.

8
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:27,764
He points, and gives quick orders.

9
00:00:28,114 --> 00:00:29,014
Asha brews.

10
00:00:29,010 --> 00:00:30,558
Kabir takes the money.

11
00:00:30,558 --> 00:00:32,807
Meena keeps the clean cups coming.

12
00:00:33,157 --> 00:00:36,125
The queue moves, and every
customer leaves smiling.

13
00:00:36,775 --> 00:00:38,616
There is no one best way.

14
00:00:38,616 --> 00:00:39,784
It all depends.

15
00:00:40,484 --> 00:00:42,760
This is the contingency approach.

16
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:45,534
Contingent means depending on something.

17
00:00:45,884 --> 00:00:50,448
Earlier thinkers, like Taylor, searched
for one best way for every job.

18
00:00:50,798 --> 00:00:55,014
This approach says the right way
to manage depends on the situation.

19
00:00:55,664 --> 00:00:59,016
The idea grew from research
in the nineteen sixties.

20
00:00:59,366 --> 00:01:04,837
In 1961, Burns and Stalker found
that firms in a stable market do well

21
00:01:04,837 --> 00:01:06,458
with strict rules.

22
00:01:06,808 --> 00:01:10,603
But firms in a fast changing
market need flexible teams.

23
00:01:10,953 --> 00:01:15,010
In 1965, Joan Woodward
studied one hundred factories.

24
00:01:15,010 --> 00:01:18,589
The best structure depended
on their technology.

25
00:01:18,939 --> 00:01:24,032
In 1967, Lawrence and Lorsch showed
that it depends on the environment,

26
00:01:24,032 --> 00:01:24,932
too.

27
00:01:24,957 --> 00:01:29,346
And Fred Fiedler showed that the
best leader depends on the situation.

28
00:01:29,996 --> 00:01:32,439
So Ravi writes himself a simple rule.

29
00:01:32,439 --> 00:01:33,931
If this, then that.

30
00:01:34,281 --> 00:01:38,115
If the stall is quiet, then the
team chats and shares ideas.

31
00:01:38,465 --> 00:01:41,551
If the queue is long, then
he gives quick, firm orders.

32
00:01:41,901 --> 00:01:44,622
Watch the same morning rush, run two ways.

33
00:01:44,972 --> 00:01:47,145
With a team chat, nobody is serving.

34
00:01:47,145 --> 00:01:50,012
The queue grows, and the
wait hits twelve minutes.

35
00:01:50,362 --> 00:01:54,335
With firm orders, everyone has one
job, and the queue keeps moving.

36
00:01:54,685 --> 00:01:56,610
The wait drops to three minutes.

37
00:01:56,610 --> 00:01:58,937
Same team, same stall, a better fit.

38
00:01:59,587 --> 00:02:02,052
So what does the right way depend on?

39
00:02:02,052 --> 00:02:03,228
Four big things.

40
00:02:03,578 --> 00:02:04,478
Size.

41
00:02:04,213 --> 00:02:07,183
Six people need fewer rules than sixty.

42
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:08,433
Technology.

43
00:02:08,414 --> 00:02:11,260
That means the tools and methods you use.

44
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:12,607
Environment.

45
00:02:12,607 --> 00:02:14,841
Customers, prices and rivals.

46
00:02:14,841 --> 00:02:17,316
Is it stable, or changing fast?

47
00:02:17,666 --> 00:02:18,609
And people.

48
00:02:18,609 --> 00:02:20,823
A new helper needs clear steps.

49
00:02:20,823 --> 00:02:22,556
An expert needs freedom.

50
00:02:23,206 --> 00:02:25,684
So what does this look like at work today?

51
00:02:26,034 --> 00:02:26,934
Structure.

52
00:02:26,893 --> 00:02:29,301
A big bank runs on strict rules.

53
00:02:29,301 --> 00:02:31,528
A small startup stays flexible.

54
00:02:31,878 --> 00:02:32,778
Leadership.

55
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:36,772
In an emergency room, the lead
doctor gives quick, firm orders.

56
00:02:37,122 --> 00:02:38,022
Planning.

57
00:02:37,926 --> 00:02:42,644
A phone maker changes its plans
often, because technology moves fast.

58
00:02:42,994 --> 00:02:43,894
People.

59
00:02:43,658 --> 00:02:45,883
A new nurse follows a checklist.

60
00:02:45,883 --> 00:02:48,370
A senior nurse is trusted to decide.

61
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:51,146
So is it depends a good answer?

62
00:02:51,146 --> 00:02:52,966
Mostly yes, with a catch.

63
00:02:53,316 --> 00:02:54,432
The good side.

64
00:02:54,432 --> 00:02:57,782
It fits real life, and it
keeps managers flexible.

65
00:02:58,132 --> 00:02:59,316
The hard side.

66
00:02:59,316 --> 00:03:01,202
It gives no ready answer.

67
00:03:01,202 --> 00:03:03,675
Every situation must be read first.

68
00:03:04,025 --> 00:03:08,664
With so many factors, a manager can
read it wrong, or decide too slowly.

69
00:03:09,014 --> 00:03:13,113
Critics also say it shows what
works, but rarely explains why.

70
00:03:13,463 --> 00:03:15,155
So the lesson is simple.

71
00:03:15,155 --> 00:03:17,883
Learn the tools, then read the situation.

72
00:03:18,533 --> 00:03:19,433
Quick check.

73
00:03:19,412 --> 00:03:21,969
Festival day, thirty people in the queue.

74
00:03:21,969 --> 00:03:23,973
Which style fits best right now?

75
00:03:25,873 --> 00:03:27,212
Quick, firm orders!

76
00:03:27,212 --> 00:03:29,591
A rush needs speed, not a long chat.

77
00:03:30,191 --> 00:03:31,204
So, remember.

78
00:03:31,504 --> 00:03:33,915
There is no one best way to manage.

79
00:03:33,915 --> 00:03:36,063
It all depends on the situation.

80
00:03:36,413 --> 00:03:40,585
Size, technology, environment and
people shape the right choice.

81
00:03:40,935 --> 00:03:42,163
And think if-then.

82
00:03:42,163 --> 00:03:44,965
Read the situation,
then pick the approach.

83
00:03:45,565 --> 00:03:49,713
Next time, we'll explore the core
jobs of every manager, the functions

84
00:03:49,713 --> 00:03:50,717
of management.
